## Every Cert in the Project

### 1. FreeIPA CA Cert — `/etc/ipa/ca.crt`

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | CA certificate (verifier, not identity) |
| **Issued by** | FreeIPA CA (self-signed root) |
| **Lives on** | Every domain-joined node — vault1/2/3, k8s master/workers, ansible node |
| **Same content?** | Yes — identical file on all nodes |
| **Purpose** | Lets any domain-joined node verify a cert signed by FreeIPA |
| **Used by** | Vault (tls_client_ca_file), vault-injector sidecar, anything calling vault.lab.local |

This is NOT anyone's identity. It's just the reference copy of the CA's public cert. Having this file
means "I can verify certificates signed by FreeIPA CA."

---

### 2. Vault Service Cert — `/opt/vault/tls/tls.crt` + `/opt/vault/tls/tls.key`

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | Server identity certificate |
| **Issued by** | FreeIPA CA (via `ipa-getcert request`) |
| **Lives on** | Each vault node separately (vault1, vault2, vault3) |
| **Same content?** | No — each node has its own cert with its own identity |
| **Managed by** | Certmonger (`ipa-getcert`) — auto-renews before expiry |
| **Validity** | 2 years (FreeIPA default), auto-renewed |

**What's in the SANs:**
- `vault1.lab.local` (its own hostname)
- `vault.lab.local` (the VIP — added after the TS-VLT-002 incident)
- Kerberos UPN: `vault/vault1.lab.local@LAB.LOCAL`

**Why vault.lab.local is in the SAN:** When vault-injector (running in K8s) calls
`https://vault.lab.local:8200`, it verifies the presented cert against the hostname it's connecting to.
If `vault.lab.local` is not in the SAN, TLS fails with "no alternative certificate subject name
matches." Had to add it explicitly after the VIP was introduced.

**How it was added:** Required two `managedby` relationships in FreeIPA — both host managedby AND
service managedby for vault1/2/3 on the VIP entries. FreeIPA won't sign a cert with a SAN unless the
requesting host has explicit permission for that name.

**Private key:** `/opt/vault/tls/tls.key` — chmod 600, owned by vault user. Never leaves the node.
Never shared. This is what proves vault1 actually owns the cert when someone connects.

**After renewal:** Certmonger runs `systemctl reload vault` automatically so Vault picks up the new
cert without manual intervention.

**Used by:** Vault listener (`tls_cert_file`), Raft cluster communication between vault nodes, anything
connecting to Vault over HTTPS.

---

### 3. K8s Internal CA — `/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt`

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | CA certificate (verifier, not identity) |
| **Issued by** | kubeadm (self-signed, generated at `kubeadm init`) |
| **Lives on** | Master nodes and distributed to workers during join |
| **Purpose** | Lets K8s components verify each other's certs |
| **Used by** | Vault — passed as `kubernetes_ca_cert` in `auth/kubernetes/config` |

This is the CA cert Vault needs to trust K8s API's TLS when it calls the TokenReview endpoint.
When Vault calls `https://10.0.51.100:16443`, K8s API presents its server cert (signed by this CA).
Without this CA cert, Vault can't verify it's actually talking to K8s — TLS handshake fails.

Nothing to do with FreeIPA. These are two separate trust domains.

---

### 4. K8s API Server Cert — `/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt`

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | Server identity certificate |
| **Issued by** | K8s internal CA |
| **Lives on** | Master nodes |
| **Purpose** | Proves to clients that this is the real K8s API server |
| **Used by** | kubectl, vault, kubelet — anyone calling K8s API |

Not directly referenced in project config but it's what K8s API presents during TLS handshakes.

---

### 5. etcd Certs — `/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/`

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | Multiple — CA + peer certs + client certs |
| **Issued by** | etcd CA (separate from K8s CA, also by kubeadm) |
| **Lives on** | Master nodes |
| **Purpose** | etcd peer-to-peer communication + client access to etcd |

**Files that matter for etcd backup:**
- `/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt` — the etcd CA cert
- `/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt` + `server.key` — used by the backup job to connect to etcd

The etcd backup CronJob runs on a master node with `hostPath` access to these files. Without them,
`etcdctl` can't authenticate to etcd.

---

### 6. vault-ca K8s Secret — Per Namespace

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | K8s Secret containing the IPA CA cert content |
| **Source** | Copy of `/etc/ipa/ca.crt` |
| **Lives on** | Every namespace that runs Vault-injected pods |
| **Purpose** | Lets vault-agent sidecar verify Vault's TLS cert |

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: vault-ca
  namespace: <namespace>
type: Opaque
stringData:
  ca.crt: |
    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
    <FreeIPA CA certificate content>
    -----END CERTIFICATE-----
```

The vault-agent sidecar runs inside K8s pods. When it calls `https://vault.lab.local:8200`, Vault
presents its TLS cert (signed by IPA CA). The sidecar needs to verify that cert. Since it's inside a
container, it doesn't automatically have `/etc/ipa/ca.crt`. The `vault-ca` Secret is how the IPA CA
cert gets mounted into the sidecar.

Referenced via annotations:
```yaml
vault.hashicorp.com/tls-secret: "vault-ca"       # mounts the Secret at /vault/tls/
vault.hashicorp.com/ca-cert: "/vault/tls/ca.crt"  # tells agent where the CA cert is
```

**Must exist in every namespace** where vault injection is used. Each app namespace has its own copy:
`vault-ca-secret.yaml` per namespace (wordpress, mariadb, monitoring, alertmanager, remediation,
etcd-backup, nginx-test).

