## The Two TLS Connections — Side by Side

During the K8s ↔ Vault runtime flow, two separate HTTPS connections happen:

```
Connection 1: vault-injector (in K8s pod) → Vault cluster
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Direction:    K8s → Vault
URL:          https://vault.lab.local:8200
Vault presents: /opt/vault/tls/tls.crt (IPA-signed, covers vault.lab.local SAN)
Injector verifies using: /etc/ipa/ca.crt (mounted from vault-ca K8s Secret)
Result: "yes, this is really vault.lab.local and I trust it"

Connection 2: Vault → K8s API (TokenReview)
────────────────────────────────────────────
Direction:    Vault → K8s
URL:          https://10.0.51.100:16443
K8s presents: apiserver.crt (K8s internal CA-signed)
Vault verifies using: kubernetes_ca_cert (K8s CA cert, passed at trust setup)
Result: "yes, this is really the K8s API and I trust it"
```

Different CAs, different directions, different purposes. They happen in sequence during every pod
authentication event.

