## Quick Reference — Which Cert For What

| I need to... | Cert I need |
|---|---|
| Verify something signed by FreeIPA | `/etc/ipa/ca.crt` |
| Prove I am vault1 over HTTPS | `/opt/vault/tls/tls.crt` + `tls.key` |
| Connect to Vault API and verify it | Need `/etc/ipa/ca.crt` (it trusts vault's cert) |
| Have Vault trust K8s API | Pass K8s CA cert in `auth/kubernetes/config` |
| Run vault-agent sidecar in a pod | Create `vault-ca` K8s Secret with IPA CA cert content |
| Backup etcd directly | Need etcd CA + server cert from `/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/` |
| K8s nodes talk to each other | K8s internal CA handles it automatically (kubeadm) |

---

## What "Same CA Cert" Means and Doesn't Mean

All domain-joined nodes having `/etc/ipa/ca.crt` with the same content does NOT mean they share an
identity. It means they all have the same reference to verify IPA-signed certs.

Analogy: everyone in a country has the same government that issues passports. Knowing the government
exists doesn't mean everyone has the same passport. The CA cert is knowledge of the government. The
service cert is the individual passport.

Each vault node has its own cert (`tls.crt`) with its own identity, signed by that same CA. The certs
are different — only the CA cert that verifies them is the same.